# Day 23 – Introduction to Containers

## Overview

- What containers are  
- Why they were introduced  
- How they differ from virtual machines  
- The basic lifecycle of Docker  
- Introduction to Builder  

This is a foundational explanation. No commands or projects are implemented here.

---

## Virtual Machines – The Foundation

Containers are an advancement over virtual machines, just like virtual machines were an advancement over physical servers.

### Physical Server Problem

A physical server (or laptop) has:

- CPU  
- RAM  
- Hardware resources  

Most of the time:

- Resources are not fully utilized.  
- Organizations with thousands of servers waste resources.  

---

## Virtualization and Hypervisor

To solve this problem, virtualization was introduced.

### Hypervisor

A hypervisor allows:

- Creation of multiple virtual machines on a single physical server.  

### Virtual Machine (VM)

A virtual machine:

- Is a logical isolation.  
- Has its own operating system.  
- Runs applications on top of that OS.  

Instead of:

- One physical server → One application  

It becomes:

- One physical server → Multiple VMs → Multiple applications  

This improves resource usage and isolation.

---

## Limitations of Virtual Machines

Even with VMs:

- Resources are still underutilized.  
- Example: A VM with 25 GB RAM may only use 10 GB at peak.  
- Remaining resources are wasted.  

In large organizations using millions of instances, this becomes expensive.

This led to the introduction of containers.

---

## Containers – Evolution from Virtual Machines

Virtual machines solved problems of physical servers.

Containers solve some problems of virtual machines.

However:

- Virtual machines are more secure.  
- Containers do not provide full isolation like VMs.  

---

## Container Architecture

Containers can be created in two models.

### Model 1 – On Physical Server

Physical Server  
→ Operating System  
→ Container Platform (Docker)  
→ Containers  

### Model 2 – On Virtual Machine

Physical Server  
→ Virtual Machine (EC2/Azure VM)  
→ Container Platform (Docker)  
→ Containers  

Many organizations use Model 2 because:

- Less maintenance overhead  
- No need to maintain physical data centers  
- Cloud providers handle infrastructure  

---

## Why Containers Are Lightweight

Virtual machines:

- Have a full operating system.  
- Have complete isolation.  

Containers:

- Do not have a full operating system.  
- Use resources from the host OS.  
- Share system libraries and dependencies.  

They include:

- Application  
- Application libraries  
- System dependencies  
- Minimal base image  

They do not include a full OS like a VM.

---

## Container Package Includes

- Application  
- Application dependencies  
- System dependencies  
- Minimal base image  

---

## Image Size Comparison

Virtual Machine Snapshot:

- Usually in GB (1–3 GB or more)  

Container Image:

- Often in MB (100 MB, 500 MB, etc.)  
- Depends on base image and dependencies  

Because containers are lightweight:

- Easier to transfer  
- Easier to ship  
- Faster to deploy  

---

## Docker – Containerization Platform

Docker popularized containerization.

It simplifies:

- Creating images  
- Running containers  
- Managing lifecycle  

---

## Docker Lifecycle

### Step 1 – Write a Dockerfile

A Dockerfile defines:

- Application  
- Dependencies  
- Configuration  

### Step 2 – Build Image

Command used:

docker build

Docker Engine converts:

Dockerfile → Docker Image  

### Step 3 – Run Container

Command used:
docker run

Docker Image → Running Container  

---

## Docker Engine

Docker Engine:

- Processes Docker commands  
- Builds images  
- Runs containers  

---

## Single Point of Failure (SPOF)

Docker Engine can be:

- A Single Point of Failure  

If Docker Engine goes down:

- Containers may stop responding.  

---

## Layers in Docker

When building images:

- Docker creates multiple layers.  
- Each layer consumes storage.  

This can increase disk usage.

---

## Introduction to Builder

Builder was introduced to:

- Solve layer-related problems  
- Reduce storage issues  
- Address single point of failure challenges  
- Work well with tools like Skopeo and Podman  

### Builder Approach

Instead of writing a Dockerfile:

- A shell script can be written.  
- Builder commands can be added.  
- An image can be created.  

Builder can create:

- Docker images  
- OCI-compliant images  

---

## Key Points

- Containers evolved from virtual machines.  
- Containers are lightweight because they do not include a full OS.  
- They share host OS resources.  
- Docker simplifies the container lifecycle.  
- Docker lifecycle: Dockerfile → Image → Container  
- Docker Engine can be a single point of failure.  
- Builder is another tool for building container images.  
